Enter the number of the command you need help with: @?


@L1
        Logs off the system immediately.
@; @L2
        Returns  to  the calling menu. "Pops up" one layer  in  the  menu
        tree. Do this when you are through with the menu you're in.
@; @L3
        Same  as  PREVIOUS but pops ALL the way up to the Main
        Menu. A useful command if you are several layers deep in menus.
@; @L4
        This is where you can change your settings; linefeeds, etc.
        Allows you to alter your terminal parameters, personal  prefer-
        ences,  statistical  information and  user  biography.

        The  statistics and parameters maintained for each user  are  for
        convenience divided into two parts: the caller's personal statis-
        tics,  name, address etc., and the defaults  and  preferences
        you have set up for the actual interaction  with  Skyline.
        This is also where personal biographies are entered. None of  the
        changes are saved (except the Biography) unless you  select
        the SAVE CHANGES option on the way back to the BBS.
@; @L5
        Shows a listing of the userlog of this system. Several  different
        levels  of displayed data are available. These are chosen when
        this command is executed.
@; @L6
        This  is a message-base related command, and shows a list of  all
        the people who have JOINed this message base area. See the JOIN
        command.
@; @L7
        This  is the command that allows reading of user-written  biogra-
        phies. A list of the available user biographies is shown and  the
        user is given the opportunity to read one or more.
@; @L9
        Enters the Conference area. The Conference Facility is a set of
        "rooms" within which callers on different lines can talk to each
        other.

        @X59
@; @L10
        This is the command that lets nonmembers join the system.
@; @L12
        This pages the SYSOP for a chat. Chat hours might be restricted. It
        is possible that you will be asked for a reason.
@; @L13
        This command gives you help from any menu.
@; @L14
        Allows  a user to JOIN a message base or SIG, so that the  system
        will  allow  them to receive mail there and begin  to  track  the
        user's  use of the area. This is different than in most BBS  sys-
        tems. You can read messages in some areas even if you haven't joined
        them  and even post there, but until you JOIN it, no one  can  send
        mail  to you, and your HIGH MESSAGE READ will not be  remembered.
        Once  a message base has been JOINed it can be selected with  the
        MBase Change Area command.

        Note that some SIGS might be "locked" -- this means you MUST "Join"
        them before you can access them at all.
@; @L16
        This  is the general command to enter a message in a  message
        base. All message input, except direct replies to other messages,
        are initiated with this command.

        The user has a choice of entering his message directly or upload-
        ing it via their default transfer protocol.
@; @L18
        This  command  is  the generalized message  reading  command.  It
        serves as both a forward and reverse reading command. It asks for
        a  starting  and  an ending number for the read;  if  the  ending
        number  is higher than the starting, it reads  sequentially  for-
        ward. If not, it reads backwards.
@; @L19
        This  command  shows the user all messages entered in  this  area
        since the last one read. This works only if the caller has JOINed
        this area.
@; @L20
        This cycles through the caller's own message base list (the  ones
        he  has JOINed) and shows the new messages in each area. The caller
        may be asked before each area change if they wish to enter a message
        in this area before proceeding.
@; @L21
        This allows a search through a message base using several differ-
        ent keying possiblilties. The messages containing the search word
        in the chosen key fields will be shown sequentially.
@; @L24
        This command retrieves all messages in all areas that are direct-
        ly addressed to the user and have not been previously seen.
@; @L27
        This command is used to move an inappropriate message into anoth-
        er  area.  Often  an inxperienced user will  mistakenly  enter  a
        message  that  doesn't  belong in a certain  area.  This  command
        simply  moves it over to the new area, adjusting message  numbers
        and high-low counters appropriately.
@; @L28
        This  command turns on a specialized mode that affects  the  NEXT
        message-reading command. When activated, the results of the  next
        read  command  will be compressed in the user's  default  archive
        method  and  then  transmitted via the  user's  default  transfer
        protocol. (The default ARCHIVE method is changed with the LOGEDIT
        command;  the default protocol is selected via the PROTOCOL  com-
        mand.)
@; @L29
        This  lets the user select from among the message base  areas  he
        has  JOINed.  It will show a list of them if desired.  Note  that
        this command is also restricted by the settings in the menus.  It
        is  possible for a menu to lock out message base areas  otherwise
        allowable.
@; @L30
        This  command sends mail specifically to the SYSOP. This  is  di-
        rected   to   the SYSOP area and is usually not visible to others.
@; @L31
        This  allows  the choosing of the available  transfer  protocols.
        The default protocol then becomes  part  of the user's account and
        is used for all situations that require  a file transfer protocol.
        Your own protocol is currently set to @U32 .
@; @L33
        This command shows the new files received by the system, that are
        in areas accessible to the user, received since a specified date.
        The  default is the last day the user called. This  command  will
        show all files received on or since that day. You have a choice
        whether to view all areas or only the one you're in.
@; @L34
        This  is the standard listing of files in a database. It  is  two
        lines long and gives basic file information and a short  descrip-
        tion.
@; @L35
        This  lists the files in a database, but in an  abbreviated,
        one-line format.
@; @L36
        This is the equivalent of MBase Area Change, but in the database.
        The difference  is that users do not "join" database areas like
        they do  message base areas; all areas available by the  access
        level limitations and the menu limitations are available to the
        user.
@; @L37
        This  option  controls the Marked Files list. While  viewing  the
        database  listings,  a user can choose to MARK a file  for  later
        downloading. When the listings stop after each page, selecting  M
        will  allow the user to enter one or more filenumbers  for  later
        reference.  These  can then all be downloaded at  once  with  the
        DOWNLOAD command. The MARKFILES command allows the user to delete
        one  or  more files from their Marked Files list,  or  clear  the
        whole list.
@; @L38
        This  option will search through either one or all databases  for
        matches, either in the name or description, of a chosen keyword.
@; @L39
        This  command  lists everything that is known  about  this  file,
        including the long description if any.
@; @L40
        This is a listing command that lets you set the displayed list up
        practically  any  way you want. You can  choose  practically  any
        meaningful combination of New, Global, Short list, Expanded list,
        Continuous,  Files addressed to you, Files uploaded by  you,  and
        Browse mode. This is often handy in specialized situations.
@; @L41
        This  command,  intended for SIGOPS and SYSOPS only,  allows  the
        changing of a database listing.
@; @L42
        The  generalized  UPLOAD command. The appropriate  protocols  and
        prompts are given and the transfer is begun.

        The  user  has a choice of entering the  descriptions  before  or
        after  the transmission. If the descriptions are  entered  first,
        they must be in the same order that the files will be sent. After
        the (possibly batch) transfer, any files that do not have  accom-
        panying descriptions will be discarded.
@; @L43
        This  option is simply a combination of UPLOAD and  LOGOFF.  This
        will  often be used with pre-written descriptions,  allowing  the
        user  to begin the transfer and leave, as can be done with  down-
        loading.  After the transfer, the caller is given 10  seconds  to
        abort  the  disconnection by pressing a key. Pressing G  at  this
        time will log off immediately.
@; @L44
        Initiates the chosen download, single or batch, using the default
        protocol.
@; @L45
        Exactly the same function as a DOWNLOAD followed by LOGOFF. After
        the  transfer, the system gives the user 10 seconds to  interrupt
        the  logoff  by pressing a key. They can also press G  to  logoff
        immediately.
@; @L46
        Allows  a  file in an inappropriate database to be moved  to  the
        correct  one.
@; @L47
        Delete  one  or more database files.
@; @L49
        This  puts all the database listing commands into  a  continuous-
        output  mode. Selecting this command again turns this mode  off.
        This  is useful for getting capture file dumps of  listings.  The
        setting is "sticky", that is, it is remembered on your next call.
@; @L53
        This  allows a one-line message to be transmitted to one or  more
        people  on other lines. A common use for this is to ask if  those
        other users want to meet in a conference room.
@; @L55
        This  command toggles whether the user wants his message  reading
        commands to work without interruption, for instance for a  buffer
        capture, or whether he wants to interact normally with them. This
        choice  is  saved in the user's account and is  remembered  until
        changed.
@; @L56
        When this mode is on, listings in the database will be shown  one
        at  a time, with full descriptions, and the option to  mark  them
        for later downloading.
@; @L57
        This  shows the names of people on other lines, and whether  they
        are  available  for messages or other interactions.  Users  whose
        privilege  flags have the INVISIBLE flag set will not be seen  by
        this command or any other line-information command.
@; @L58
        This  displays a list of the last ten callers to the system,  all
        lines included. This command is often used in an embedded command
        during the logon sequence.
@; @L59
        This  command lists the names of the people who are currently  in
        the conference rooms, and where they are.
@;
